          <a href="#compare" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Compared With Other Appliances</a>
          <a href="#real-cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. The Bigger Expense</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most home air purifiers use roughly 5 to 50 watts, depending on size and fan speed, with large-room models drawing around 60 to 100 watts or more on their top setting. A unit averaging 30 watts around the clock uses about 22 kilowatt-hours a month, which is roughly $3.45 to $3.70 at 16 to 17 cents per kilowatt-hour, around the US average; check your own rate on your bill. That makes the electricity a minor line item for most households. Fan speed decides almost all of it, standby power is close to negligible, and over a year the replacement filters usually cost more than the power.
        </p>

        <div id="watts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Many Watts Does an Air Purifier Draw?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Less than a laptop on most settings. An air purifier is essentially a fan pushing air through a filter, and fans are not big power users. The exact draw depends on two things: the size of the unit and the speed it is running at. The table below shows typical ranges; your model's manual or spec sheet lists its own figures, often for each speed.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Purifier Size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Lowest Speed</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Highest Speed</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Small desktop or bedside</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2 to 8 W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 15 to 30 W</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mid-size bedroom or living room</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 4 to 15 W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30 to 60 W</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Large room or open plan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 8 to 20 W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 60 to 120 W</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Speed matters far more than size. Fan power rises much faster than airflow: pushing a fan from medium to high might add a third more air but double the watts or more. That is why the same purifier can sip 6 watts on its sleep setting and pull 50 on turbo. Most units spend most of their time on the lower speeds, especially in auto mode.
          </p>

          <p>
            Motor type plays a part as well. Many newer purifiers use brushless DC motors, which are generally more efficient at low speeds than the older AC motors in cheaper units. Features like Wi-Fi, displays and air quality sensors add a watt or two at most.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want the real number for your own unit, a plug-in watt meter costs less than a replacement filter and shows the draw at each speed. It is the easiest way to stop guessing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Much Does It Cost to Run Per Month?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The math is simple. Multiply the watts by the hours it runs, divide by 1,000 to get kilowatt-hours, and multiply by your electricity rate. A 30 watt purifier running 24 hours a day for 30 days uses 30 times 720 hours, or 21,600 watt-hours, which is 21.6 kilowatt-hours. At 16.5 cents per kilowatt-hour, that is about $3.56 for the month.
          </p>

          <p>
            We use 16.5 cents because it sits around the recent US residential average, but rates vary enormously by state and by plan, from well under that in some states to more than double in others. Your bill lists your rate per kilowatt-hour; use that number, and include delivery charges if your utility bills them per kilowatt-hour too.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Average Draw</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">kWh per Month (24/7)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Monthly Cost at 16.5 Cents</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Yearly Cost</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5 W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3.6 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.60</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $7</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">10 W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7.2 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.20</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $14</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">20 W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">14.4 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.40</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $29</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">30 W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21.6 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $3.55</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $43</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">50 W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">36 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $5.95</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $72</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">100 W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">72 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $11.90</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $143</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Note the word average. Few purifiers run at one speed all day. A unit on auto mode might spend 20 hours on low at 8 watts and 4 hours on medium at 25 watts, which averages out to around 11 watts and costs roughly $1.30 a month. Only a purifier left on its highest setting all day and night gets near the bottom rows of that table.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most households, then, running a purifier continuously costs somewhere between about $1 and $6 a month. That is the main reason we recommend leaving it on rather than switching it off to save money.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is It Cheaper to Run a Bigger Purifier on Low?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Often, yes, and it is quieter too. A larger purifier running on low can deliver the same clean air as a small one running flat out, while drawing similar or fewer watts, because a bigger, slower fan moves air more efficiently than a small one spinning hard. The small unit on high is also the one you will turn off at night because of the noise.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is the strongest argument for buying a size up. Choose a purifier whose clean air delivery rate, or CADR, comfortably exceeds what your room needs, then run it at a medium or low setting. You get enough air changes per hour to make a difference, at a power draw and noise level you can live with. Our guide to <a href="/what-size-air-purifier-do-i-need/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">what size air purifier you need</a> shows how to work out the CADR your room needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Auto mode is usually the most efficient way to run any purifier. It sits on low most of the time and ramps up only when the sensor detects a spike from cooking, dust or smoke. That gives you high-speed cleaning when it matters and low-speed power use the rest of the day.
          </p>

          <p>
            The one setting worth avoiding as a default is turbo. It is useful for clearing a room quickly after cooking or during wildfire smoke, but as an all-day setting it costs the most, wears filters fastest and is loud enough that most people end up switching the unit off altogether, which defeats the point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="energy-star" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Does an Energy Star Air Purifier Save Much?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It helps, mainly by ruling out the inefficient units. ENERGY STAR certified room air cleaners have to meet a minimum efficiency measured as clean air delivered per watt, using the same CADR testing that appears on the box, and the program also caps standby power. Certification tells you the purifier is not wasting electricity to move its air.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can do a rough version of that check yourself. Divide the smoke CADR by the watts on the highest speed. A unit delivering 200 cubic feet per minute at 50 watts manages 4 CFM per watt; one delivering the same 200 at 100 watts manages 2. Over a year of continuous use, the more efficient one saves real money, though usually tens of dollars rather than hundreds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because the totals are small, efficiency should be a tie-breaker, not the main reason to pick a purifier. Filter quality, a properly sealed housing, honest CADR, noise and the price of replacement filters matter more to how well and how cheaply the unit does its job. Between two otherwise similar models, take the certified one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be wary of efficiency claims without numbers. A listing that says energy saving without stating watts per speed, or a CADR from an independent test, tells you nothing. Look for the AHAM Verified mark for CADR and the ENERGY STAR label, or the watts in the manual.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="standby" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much Power Does It Use in Standby?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Very little. Most purifiers draw well under 2 watts when switched off but still plugged in, and many draw under 1 watt. At 1 watt, a full year of standby uses about 8.8 kilowatt-hours, which is around $1.50 at a typical US rate. Unplugging a purifier to save standby power is not worth the effort.
          </p>

          <p>
            Smart features add a little to that. A purifier with Wi-Fi keeps its radio connected so the app can reach it, and some keep a display lit or an air quality sensor running. That might add a watt or two. It is still pennies a month, and dimming or turning off the display at night, which most units allow, is more about light in the bedroom than about the bill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ionizer and UV features use small amounts of power too, typically a few watts. They do not add much to the electricity cost, but they are not free in other ways: some ionizing technologies produce ozone, and small UV lamps do little for air cleaning. Our guide to <a href="/do-air-purifiers-produce-ozone/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether air purifiers produce ozone</a> covers which features to leave switched off.
          </p>

          <p>
            The real standby question is whether the purifier should be off at all. In most homes the answer is no, because the electricity saved by switching it off is tiny next to the benefit of keeping the air clean. Our guide on <a href="/should-you-run-an-air-purifier-24-7/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether to run an air purifier 24/7</a> goes through the exceptions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Does an Air Purifier Compare With Other Appliances?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It is one of the cheaper appliances to run continuously. On a typical low or auto setting it uses about the same power as one or two LED bulbs. Even on high, a mid-size unit draws far less than anything that heats, cools or removes moisture, because moving air takes much less energy than changing its temperature or wringing water out of it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Appliance</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Draw While Running</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">LED light bulb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 8 to 12 W</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Air purifier on low or auto</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 5 to 20 W</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ceiling fan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 15 to 75 W</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Air purifier on high</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30 to 100 W</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Basement dehumidifier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 300 to 700 W</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Window air conditioner</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 500 to 1,500 W</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Portable space heater on high</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1,500 W</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The comparison that surprises people is the dehumidifier. A typical basement dehumidifier draws several hundred watts while it runs, which can be ten to fifty times what a purifier uses. If you are running both to deal with a damp basement, the dehumidifier is where nearly all of the electricity goes. Our breakdown of <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how much electricity a dehumidifier uses</a> has the numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Likewise, a space heater on high uses as much power in one hour as a 30 watt purifier uses in two days. If you are trying to bring down your bill, the purifier is almost never the place to start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="real-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Does an Air Purifier Really Cost to Own?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Filters, mostly. A replacement HEPA filter for a mainstream purifier often costs somewhere around $20 to $100 or more depending on size and brand, and most need replacing every 6 to 12 months. Units with separate carbon filters need those replaced more often, typically every 3 to 6 months. For many purifiers, a year of filters costs more than a year of electricity, sometimes several times more.
          </p>

          <p>
            That changes how to shop. Before buying, look up the price and availability of the replacement filter set and multiply it by how often the maker says to change it. A cheap purifier with expensive or hard-to-find filters can end up costing more over three years than a pricier one with affordable filters. Our guide to <a href="/how-often-to-change-air-purifier-filter/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how often to change an air purifier filter</a> covers the realistic schedule.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running the purifier continuously does wear filters faster than running it a few hours a day. That is a real cost, but it is the cost of the purifier actually doing its job. Pre-filters you vacuum or rinse every few weeks extend the life of the HEPA layer, which is the cheapest maintenance you can do.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put it together and a typical mid-size purifier running around the clock costs roughly $15 to $70 a year in electricity, plus its filters. Our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-home/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best air purifiers for home</a> factor in efficiency and filter cost as well as CADR and noise, so the purchase price is not the only number you are looking at.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it on turbo all day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The highest speed can draw several times the power of the lowest while adding much less airflow, and it is loud enough that most people end up switching it off. Use high to clear the room quickly, then auto or low to keep it clean, and size the unit so that low is enough.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a national average instead of your rate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Electricity prices vary widely by state, plan and time of day. A purifier that costs $3 a month at an average rate can cost twice that somewhere expensive. Take the per-kilowatt-hour figure from your own bill, including delivery charges, and run the math with that.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Switching it off to save money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Turning off a purifier that costs a few dollars a month means the air in the room returns to where it started within an hour or two. The savings are small and the benefit disappears. Leave it on auto, which already keeps power low when the air is clean.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring filter prices</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Electricity is usually the smaller running cost. Replacement HEPA and carbon filters can cost more per year than the power. Check what filters cost and how often they are due before you buy, and avoid models whose filters are expensive, proprietary and hard to find.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a small unit to save power</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A small purifier running on high to keep up with a room often uses as much power as a larger one on low, and it is louder and less effective. Size for the room's CADR needs, then run it slowly, which is usually more efficient and much quieter.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does an air purifier use more electricity than a fan?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About the same. A purifier is a fan that pushes air through a filter, so it uses a similar amount of power to a box fan or ceiling fan at comparable speeds, and sometimes a bit more because the filter adds resistance. Both are small loads next to air conditioners, heaters and dehumidifiers, and both cost a few dollars a month to run.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it expensive to run an air purifier 24/7?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not usually. Most home purifiers running continuously on auto or a low setting cost roughly $1 to $4 a month at around the US average electricity rate, and a large unit on high all day might reach $10 or more. Check your own per-kilowatt-hour rate, and remember that filter replacement is often the larger annual cost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many kWh does an air purifier use per year?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A purifier averaging 10 watts uses about 88 kilowatt-hours a year if it runs nonstop, one averaging 30 watts about 263, and one averaging 50 watts about 438. Multiply the average watts by 8.76 to get the yearly figure, then multiply by your rate in dollars per kilowatt-hour to get the annual cost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do air purifiers use a lot of power on sleep mode?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Sleep mode is typically the lowest fan speed with the display dimmed, and most purifiers draw only a few watts in it, often under 10. That makes overnight running one of the cheapest things you can leave on. The catch is that sleep mode also delivers the least clean air, so the unit needs to be sized generously.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will an air purifier raise my electric bill noticeably?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most households, no. A few dollars a month is usually lost in the normal variation of a bill. The exception is several large purifiers running on high around the clock, which can add $20 or $30 a month. If your bill jumped after buying a purifier, a heater, air conditioner or dehumidifier is the more likely cause.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are ionizer purifiers cheaper to run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They draw little power and have no HEPA filter to replace, so their running costs are low. But some ionizing and electrostatic technologies produce ozone, a lung irritant, and they tend to deposit particles on nearby walls rather than capturing them. If you buy one, check that it is certified by the California Air Resources Board for ozone emissions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a dirty filter make an air purifier use more electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not much, and sometimes slightly less, because a clogged filter reduces how much air the fan moves. The real cost is performance: less clean air for the same running time. On some units with sensors, a dirty filter can keep auto mode running at higher speeds for longer. Change filters on schedule for the clean air, not the electricity.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

