        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            An air purifier is a fan with a filter, and it uses power like one: a handful of watts on low, a few dozen on high, and a few dollars a month for most homes running one continuously. Do the math with your own rate if you want the exact figure, but for almost everyone the electricity is not a reason to switch it off.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The numbers worth watching are the filter price and the CADR. Buy a unit big enough to clean your room on a quiet setting, check what its filters cost before you buy, and let auto mode handle the rest. That is the cheapest way to run one, and the most effective.
          </p>
